Content-Type: text/plain; charset="us-ascii"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

All,

Attached is an article on the subject of PE fusion joint capability appeari=
ng in the June issue of Plastics Engineering.

This is a report of research performed by the nuclear industry on the capab=
ility of PE fusion joints.

It finds that the slow crack resistance of fusion joints as measured by tim=
e to failure is less than 1/10th of the pipe.  This doesn't mean that the j=
oint has 1/10th the strength of the pipe.  The PE industry misdirects by em=
phasizing strength and reports short term load test results that have nothi=
ng to do with operating conditions and life which is the real question.  Th=
e life limiting mechanism for PE pipe is the slow crack growth failure mode=
 discussed in this article and as it turns out, fusion joints are more limi=
ting than the pipe.

Many things can reduce the slow crack resistance (and thus life) of PE incl=
uding elevated temperature, stress concentrations, surge stresses, and chem=
ically induced degradation (such as chlorine).  It is stress concentrations=
 due to dissimilar DR fusion joints that caused the slow crack growth failu=
res in Florida and elsewhere referred to as "Polygate".

Joints at fittings will be the most limiting.  Saddle and socket joints wil=
l be more limiting than butt fusion joints because of their states of stres=
s.

The joints have less slow crack resistance because the fusion process chang=
es the molecular structure of the material presumably.

This doesn't mean that the joints won't last for years however there is no =
method to rationally design a system for considerations of the fusion joint=
s.

PVC doesn't have this issue by the way.

John

